    "result_text": "Green. Here's what I implemented.\n\n## Change\n\n**Feature \u2014 cap membership level by household** (`owners.POST` pipeline). Added a new step `CapMembershipLevel` that runs right after `AssignMembershipLevel`:\n\n- `src/main/java/.../owner/CapMembershipLevel.java` \u2014 reads the raw level just assigned, computes the household (`HouseholdId` over last name + postcode, same rule as `CountHousehold`/`RejectDuplicateOwner`), finds the max `membershipLevel` among existing household members (via `OwnerRepository.findAll()`, which pre-save sees only prior owners), and caps the new owner's level to `maxExisting + 1`. With no existing household member, the level is left unchanged.\n- `src/main/resources/officefloor/rest/api/owners.POST.yml` \u2014 wired `capMembershipLevel` between `assignMembershipLevel` and `flagBulkSignup`, with `govern: [ transaction ]` like its neighbours.\n\n**Prerequisite fix \u2014 `RejectDuplicateOwner`.** The new acceptance test creates two owners in the *same household* with different telephones, and both must be created for the cap to be observable. The duplicate block was rejecting on household match alone, which contradicted both its own `DuplicateIdentityException` (\"request's derived IdentityKey collides\u2026 replaces the former separate telephone, email and household duplicate checks\") and `IdentityKey`'s javadoc (\"two members of the same household with different telephones derive different identityKeys\"). I changed it to reject only on a full `identityKey` collision (telephone|email|household), so distinct household members coexist while genuine identity duplicates are still 409'd. Updated its class javadoc to match.\n\n## Verification\n\n`AssignMembershipLevel` naturally gave owner B level 2 and A level 1 (audit log: `id=11 membershipLevel=1`, `id=12 membershipLevel=2`), satisfying `lb <= la + 1`. Full suite: **1 run, 0 failures, BUILD SUCCESS.**\n\nOne thing worth flagging: relaxing `RejectDuplicateOwner` from household-based to identityKey-based rejection is a behavioral change beyond the literal cap spec \u2014 I made it because the provided test requires two same-household owners to both be created (without setting `sharesHousehold`), and it aligns the code with its own documented intent. If you'd rather keep the strict household-duplicate rule and instead have the test declare `sharesHousehold: true`, let me know and I'll revert that part.",
